<h2>Holding one: registration must precede the contract</h2>
<p>The MSMED Act's delayed-payment machinery — the forty-five-day ceiling in Section 15, compound interest at three times the bank rate under Section 16, and the Section 18 reference — is available to a "supplier", a status tied to the enterprise's registration under the Act. In Silpi Industries the Court held that the seller should have been registered as on the date of entering into the contract to claim these benefits; registration obtained after supplies had commenced could not be projected backwards.</p>
<p>The logic is contractual fairness. The Act imposes onerous, non-negotiable terms on buyers — an interest rate designed to sting and a compressed credit period — and a buyer is entitled to know, when it contracts, whether it is dealing with a protected supplier. Allowing retrospective registration would change the bargain after the fact.</p>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Register first, contract second: an enterprise intending to rely on the Act should complete Udyam registration before accepting purchase orders.</li>
  <li>For long-running relationships, benefits attach to contracts and supplies made after registration; the reference should be framed accordingly.</li>
  <li>Buyers facing references should verify the supplier's registration date against the contract date at the outset — it is a threshold defence.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Holding two: counterclaims are maintainable</h2>
<p>The second question was procedural but consequential: when a supplier drags a buyer to the Facilitation Council, can the buyer assert its own cross-claims — for defective goods, delayed delivery, risk-purchase costs, or recovery of advances — in the same arbitration? The Court held that a counterclaim is maintainable before the statutory forum. Section 18(3) applies the Arbitration and Conciliation Act to the Council's arbitration as if it were consensual, and with it comes Section 23(2A), which expressly permits counterclaims and set-offs within the scope of the arbitration.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Without counterclaims</h3>
    <p>The Council decides the supplier's invoices; the buyer's cross-claims go to a separate suit or arbitration — two forums, two timelines, and the risk of inconsistent findings on one transaction.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>After Silpi Industries</h3>
    <p>One tribunal decides the whole account: claim, set-off and counterclaim. The award nets the positions, and any challenge proceeds under the Arbitration Act with the Section 19 pre-deposit protecting the supplier.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>How the two holdings interact in practice</h2>
<p>A well-advised reference now anticipates both rules. The supplier pleads its registration date and annexes the certificate, frames the claim invoice-wise within limitation, and prepares for the buyer's counterclaim on the merits rather than objecting to its maintainability. The buyer, conversely, has every incentive to bring its genuine cross-claims into the Council proceeding — supported by contemporaneous rejection memos, inspection reports and correspondence — since a counterclaim raised for the first time as an afterthought in Section 34 proceedings will not be entertained.</p>
<p>For Delhi practice, references concerning supplies to buyers in the capital proceed before the Delhi Micro and Small Enterprises Facilitation Council, and awards are executable as decrees. The strategic centre of gravity has shifted to the Council stage itself: with limitation, registration and counterclaims all justiciable there, the arbitral record — not the later challenge — usually decides the dispute.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Note:</strong> This article completes the chamber's coverage of Silpi Industries; the limitation holding is discussed separately. General information only, not legal advice.</p>
</div>
